\section{Substituting Atoms in Shared Pair Structure}

Symbolic substitution gives a smaller model of the operations that
made S-expressions useful for algebra. Here the input is a finite,
acyclic graph of immutable pairs, a distinguished symbol to replace,
and a replacement atom. This is a current implementation exercise,
not a transcription of McCarthy's evaluator. Traverse both fields
of every pair, including dotted tails, and replace only atoms
identical to the distinguished symbol. The output is a new root
with all selected occurrences replaced; untouched pairs can be
reused, and a shared input pair yields a shared output pair.
The walker never interprets a symbol as a variable or a pair as
a call. It operates even on a datum that is not legal program syntax.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input root $r$, symbols $a,b$, initially empty map $M$
  \Output root of substituted structure
  \Function{Subst}{$x$}
    \If{$x$ is an atom}
      \State \Return $b$ if $x=a$, else $x$
    \EndIf
    \If{$x$ is in $M$}
      \State \Return $M[x]$ \Comment{Key by pair identity}
    \EndIf
    \State $(h,t)\gets(\Call{Car}{x},\Call{Cdr}{x})$
    \State $(u,v)\gets(\Call{Subst}{h},\Call{Subst}{t})$
    \State $M[x]\gets x$
    \If{$(u,v)$ differs from $(h,t)$ by identity}
      \State $M[x]\gets\Call{Cons}{u,v}$ \Comment{Copy changed pair}
    \EndIf
    \State \Return $M[x]$
  \EndFunction
  \State \Return $\Call{Subst}{r}$
\end{sudocode}

Allocate $p=\texttt{(B . A)}$ and let the root be a two-element
proper list containing $p$ twice. Replace symbol \texttt{A} by
\texttt{C}. At the first visit to $p$, its head remains \texttt{B}
and its tail becomes \texttt{C}, so allocate $p'=\texttt{(B . C)}$.
When the second list element reaches $p$, the memo table already
contains $p'$. The printed result is
\verb|((B . C) (B . C))|, but one useful extra test is that its
two elements are the identical pair. Exactly three pairs are
allocated: $p'$ and the two cells on the list spine.

\Cref{fig:visual-151} makes the sharing visible. There is one
output pair \(p'\) with two incoming links, not two pairs with
equal contents.

