By induction on the graph's height, each completed memo entry
denotes the correct substitution of both of that pair's fields.
Memoization avoids expansion proportional to the unshared tree;
with hashing, the cost is expected linear time in the number of
reachable pairs. A circular input would revisit an unfinished
pair and make this procedure recurse indefinitely. Supporting
cycles would require a different construction, such as allocating
output cells before filling their fields.
